# chown — change file ownership

## Basics

```sh
chown user file
chown user:group file
chown -R user directory
chown --reference=/path/to/ref_file file    # match another file's ownership
```

## Real-world: hand the media library to Plex

```sh
sudo chown -R plex:plex /var/lib/plexmediaserver
# Media stays owned by david; make it group-readable instead
sudo chown -R david:users ~/Media
```

## Real-world: reclaim files copied in as root

```sh
sudo chown -R david:users ~/Public/joseph-journals
```

## umask — default permissions for new files

```sh
umask          # show current (e.g. 0022)
umask 0000     # reset umask
```

| umask | New file result | New dir result |
| ----- | --------------- | -------------- |
| 0002  | rw-rw-r--       | rwxrwxr-x      |
| 0022  | rw-r--r--       | rwxr-xr-x      |
| 0077  | rw-------       | rwx------      |

## Special permission bits (set with chmod)

```sh
# setuid (4000): executable runs with the owner's effective UID —
# setuid root programs run with superuser privileges
chmod u+s program        # -rwsr-xr-x

# setgid (2000): on a directory, new files inherit the directory's
# group instead of the creator's primary group — perfect for shared dirs
chmod g+s directory      # drwxrwsr-x

# sticky bit (1000): on a directory, only the file owner, dir owner,
# or root can delete/rename files (classic /tmp behavior)
chmod +t directory       # drwxrwxrwt
```

## Real-world: shared folder for a group

```sh
sudo groupadd media
sudo chown david:media ~/Media/Videos
sudo chmod 2775 ~/Media/Videos    # setgid keeps group ownership on new files
```
